// Domain 4: Identity and Access Management (20%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d4-01", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 80 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and uses an external SAML 2.0 identity provider (IdP) for its workforce. Employees must sign in to AWS with their existing corporate credentials. When an employee joins or leaves, the change must reach AWS without manual steps.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Set the external IdP as the identity source for AWS IAM Identity Center by using SAML 2.0.",
      "Turn on automatic provisioning in IAM Identity Center. Configure SCIM in the external IdP.",
      "Create an IAM user in each account for every employee. Send the passwords to each employee.",
      "Create an IAM SAML identity provider in each of the 80 accounts, with one role for each employee.",
      "Export the users from the IdP each week. Import them into IAM Identity Center by using a script."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["IAM Identity Center can use an external IdP as its identity source through SAML 2.0, so employees sign in with their corporate credentials to all accounts in the organization.",
      "With SCIM automatic provisioning, the IdP creates, updates, and removes users and groups in IAM Identity Center when employees join or leave, without manual steps.",
      "IAM users in every account create long-term credentials to manage and do not use corporate credentials.",
      "A SAML provider and roles in every account is much more overhead than IAM Identity Center.",
      "A weekly script is manual work and leaves departed employees active for up to a week."] },

{ id: "d4-02", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS IAM Identity Center for 40 AWS accounts. Database administrators need the same set of permissions in the 12 accounts that run databases, and the permissions must be updated in one place when they change.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create one permission set for the database administrators. Assign it to their group for the 12 accounts.",
      "Create an IAM role in each of the 12 accounts with the same policy. Update all 12 roles whenever needed.",
      "Create an IAM user for each administrator in each account. Attach the same policy to each of the users.",
      "Create a permission set for each account. Copy the policy into each of the 12 permission sets by hand."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A permission set defines permissions once. IAM Identity Center creates the matching roles in every account that the set is assigned to, and a change to the set is provisioned to all of those accounts.",
      "Separate roles in each account must be kept in sync by hand.",
      "IAM users in each account multiply the users and credentials to manage.",
      "Copies of the policy in separate permission sets must also be kept in sync by hand."] },

{ id: "d4-03", d: 4,
  q: "A company is building a customer-facing web application. Customers must sign up and sign in with an email address and a password. The company must require a second authentication factor, such as an authenticator app, for every customer.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use an Amazon Cognito user pool for sign-up and sign-in. Set MFA to required with TOTP software tokens.",
      "Create an IAM user for each customer. Require an MFA device for each IAM user when the user signs in.",
      "Add each customer to AWS IAM Identity Center. Require MFA every time that the customer signs in.",
      "Store the customers' password hashes in a database table. Write custom code that sends a code by email."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cognito user pools provide sign-up and sign-in for application users. A user pool can require MFA for all users, with time-based one-time password (TOTP) software tokens from authenticator apps.",
      "IAM users are for access to AWS, not for application customers, and IAM quotas do not fit a customer base.",
      "IAM Identity Center is for workforce access to AWS accounts and applications, not for customers of a web application.",
      "Custom authentication code is more work and more risk than a managed service."] },

{ id: "d4-04", d: 4,
  q: "A mobile application signs users in with an Amazon Cognito user pool. Each user must be able to upload files directly to an Amazon S3 bucket, but only under a prefix that belongs to that user. The application must not contain long-term AWS credentials.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use a Cognito identity pool to get temporary credentials, with a role policy that limits access to the ${cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub} prefix.",
      "Embed an IAM user's access keys in the application. Check in the application code that each upload uses the user's own prefix.",
      "Make the bucket public for writes. Have the application choose the prefix for each upload based on the signed-in user's name.",
      "Create one IAM role for each user of the application. Have the application assume the role that matches the signed-in user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An identity pool exchanges the user pool token for temporary AWS credentials. The role's policy can use the cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub variable, which is unique to each identity, to limit each user to the user's own prefix.",
      "Long-term keys in a mobile app can be extracted, and checks in the app can be bypassed.",
      "A public bucket lets anyone write anywhere in the bucket.",
      "A role for every user does not scale and needs a way to get credentials for the role."] },

{ id: "d4-05", d: 4,
  q: "Servers in a company's data center run scripts that call AWS APIs by using IAM user access keys. The company issues X.509 certificates to the servers from AWS Private Certificate Authority. The company must remove the long-term access keys.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an IAM Roles Anywhere trust anchor that uses the private certificate authority.",
      "Create a role that trusts rolesanywhere.amazonaws.com, and a Roles Anywhere profile for it.",
      "Store the access keys in AWS Secrets Manager. Rotate the keys every 30 days by script.",
      "Install the SSM Agent on the servers. Use the agent's role to call every other AWS API.",
      "Create an Amazon Cognito user pool. Register each server as a user with a password."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A trust anchor tells IAM Roles Anywhere which certificate authority to trust. Servers with certificates from that authority can then get temporary credentials.",
      "The role must trust the Roles Anywhere service principal, and a profile lists the roles that sessions can assume. The servers use the credential helper with their certificates instead of access keys.",
      "Rotated keys are still long-term access keys stored on the servers.",
      "Hybrid activation gives credentials for the SSM Agent's own use. It is not meant for every script on a server.",
      "Cognito user pools are for application users, and passwords are long-term secrets."] },

{ id: "d4-06", d: 4,
  q: "A company hires a third-party monitoring vendor that needs read-only access to the company's AWS account. The vendor serves many customers from its own AWS account. The company must prevent other customers of the vendor from tricking the vendor into accessing the company's account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a role that trusts the vendor's account and requires a unique external ID in the sts:ExternalId condition.",
      "Create an IAM user for the vendor with read-only permissions. Send the vendor the user's long-term access keys.",
      "Create a role that trusts the vendor's account with no conditions. Attach the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy to the role.",
      "Make the company's resources public for reading. Share the resource ARNs with the vendor's support team only."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external ID that is unique to the company prevents the confused deputy problem. The vendor must send the company's external ID when it assumes the role, so another customer cannot make the vendor use the company's role.",
      "Long-term keys shared with a vendor are hard to protect and rotate.",
      "Without an external ID condition, another customer could give the vendor the company's role ARN.",
      "Public resources are readable by anyone, not only the vendor."] },

{ id: "d4-07", d: 4,
  q: "A company's administrators use IAM users. The security team must ensure that the administrators can stop or terminate Amazon EC2 instances only when they have authenticated with MFA, including when they use the AWS CLI.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a policy statement that allows the stop and terminate actions only when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is true.",
      "Add a policy statement that allows the stop and terminate actions only when aws:SecureTransport is true.",
      "Assign an MFA device to each administrator, with no other change to the administrators' IAM policies.",
      "Turn on MFA delete for the account. Require the administrators to enter an MFA code in the AWS CLI."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent condition key is true only when the request was signed with credentials from an MFA-authenticated session. CLI users must call GetSessionToken with an MFA code to get such credentials.",
      "aws:SecureTransport checks for TLS, not MFA.",
      "An MFA device alone does not make the policies require it, and access keys still work without MFA.",
      "MFA delete is an S3 versioning feature. It does not apply to EC2 actions."] },

{ id: "d4-08", d: 4,
  q: "A company must let an external auditor download one report from a private Amazon S3 bucket. The auditor has no AWS account. Access must end automatically after 24 hours, and the bucket must stay private.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Generate a presigned URL for the object with an expiration of 24 hours by using credentials that last at least that long.",
      "Make the object public for 24 hours. Run a scheduled Lambda function that makes the object private again afterward.",
      "Create an IAM user for the auditor with access to the object. Delete the IAM user 24 hours after the user is created.",
      "Copy the report to a new public bucket. Add a lifecycle rule that deletes the copied report after one day."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A presigned URL grants time-limited access to one object by using the permissions of the person who created it. The bucket stays private, and the URL stops working when it expires.",
      "A public object can be downloaded by anyone during that time, and the clean-up depends on a function working.",
      "An IAM user creates credentials to manage and must be deleted by hand.",
      "A public bucket exposes the report to anyone who finds it."] },

{ id: "d4-09", d: 4,
  q: "A developer generates S3 presigned URLs with a 7-day expiration from a Lambda function that uses its execution role. Recipients report that the URLs stop working after a few hours.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The URLs were signed with temporary credentials, and a presigned URL stops working when those credentials expire.",
      "Presigned URLs always expire after 1 hour, whatever expiration value the developer sets for the presigned URLs.",
      "The bucket has S3 Versioning enabled, which shortens the expiration of every presigned URL that is generated for it.",
      "The recipients are opening the URLs from a different Region than the Region of the bucket that holds the objects."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A presigned URL is valid only while the credentials that signed it are valid. Role credentials, such as a Lambda execution role's, are temporary, so the URL stops working when the session expires, even if the URL's expiration is later.",
      "The expiration can be up to 7 days with long-term credentials, so there is no fixed 1-hour limit.",
      "Versioning does not affect presigned URL expiration.",
      "Presigned URLs work from any location."] },

{ id: "d4-10", d: 4,
  q: "An application on Amazon EC2 reads an Amazon SQS queue by using access keys stored in a configuration file on the instance. A security review requires that the application uses only temporary credentials that are rotated automatically.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Attach an instance profile with a role that allows the SQS actions. Remove the access keys from the file.",
      "Rotate the access keys every 30 days with a script. Store the new keys in the same configuration file.",
      "Move the access keys into the instance's user data, and read them from the metadata service at each startup.",
      "Encrypt the configuration file with an AWS KMS key, and decrypt the file each time the application starts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance profile makes role credentials available to the application through the instance metadata service. The SDK retrieves the temporary credentials and refreshes them automatically.",
      "Rotated keys are still long-term access keys on disk.",
      "User data is readable from the instance and is not a place for secrets. The keys are still long-term.",
      "Encrypting the file protects the keys at rest, but they are still long-term access keys."] },

{ id: "d4-11", d: 4,
  q: "A developer in account A has an identity policy that allows sts:AssumeRole on a role in account B. When the developer tries to assume the role, the request fails with an AccessDenied error. The role's trust policy allows only the account B root principal.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update the role's trust policy in account B to allow the account A principal that needs to assume the role.",
      "Add sts:AssumeRole to the permissions policy of the role in account B. Keep the role's trust policy as it is.",
      "Attach the AdministratorAccess policy to the developer in account A.",
      "Turn on AWS STS in the Region that the developer uses. Ask the developer to try the request again later."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account role assumption needs both sides. The caller's identity policy allows the call, and the role's trust policy must trust the caller's account or principal. Here the trust policy trusts only account B.",
      "The role's permissions policy controls what the role can do, not who can assume it.",
      "No identity policy in account A can override the trust policy in account B.",
      "STS is active in the Region, and the error comes from the trust policy."] },

{ id: "d4-12", d: 4,
  q: "A company stores its workforce identities in an on-premises Microsoft Active Directory. Employees must use their existing Active Directory credentials to sign in to AWS IAM Identity Center. The company does not want to copy the users into the cloud.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AD Connector in AWS Directory Service. Set it as the identity source for IAM Identity Center.",
      "Export the users from Active Directory. Create each user in the IAM Identity Center directory by hand.",
      "Create an IAM user for each employee, with the same user name and password as in the on-premises directory.",
      "Create an Amazon Cognito user pool. Import the Active Directory users into the pool each night by script."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AD Connector is a directory gateway that redirects sign-in requests to the on-premises Active Directory without caching or copying users into the cloud. IAM Identity Center can use it as an identity source.",
      "Creating the users by hand copies them and creates separate passwords to manage.",
      "IAM users duplicate identities and passwords, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Cognito is for application users, and nightly imports copy the users."] },

{ id: "d4-13", d: 4,
  q: "A user signs in to the AWS access portal through AWS IAM Identity Center but does not see the Production account. Other users in the user's group can see the account. The user was added to the group yesterday in the external identity provider (IdP), which uses SCIM.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The SCIM provisioning has not synced the user's new group membership from the IdP to IAM Identity Center.",
      "The Production account's SCPs deny the user's role from appearing in the AWS access portal for that account.",
      "The user must sign in with the root user of the Production account before the account appears in the portal.",
      "The permission set for the group is missing a trust policy that names the specific user who needs the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access in IAM Identity Center comes from the permission set assigned to the group for the account. If other members see the account, the assignment works, so the user's group membership has not been provisioned from the IdP through SCIM yet.",
      "SCPs affect permissions after sign-in. They do not hide an account from one user in a group.",
      "Root user sign-in has nothing to do with the access portal.",
      "IAM Identity Center creates the role trust policies itself, and they do not list individual users."] },

{ id: "d4-14", d: 4,
  q: "A company's application uses an Amazon Cognito user pool and an identity pool. Users in the user pool's Admins group must receive an IAM role with write access, and all other signed-in users must receive a read-only role.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Assign an IAM role to each user pool group. Set the identity pool to choose the role from the token.",
      "Create two identity pools, and have the application choose a pool based on the group of each signed-in user.",
      "Give every user the write role. Check in the application code whether each user is in the Admins group.",
      "Create an IAM user for each admin. Give the access keys to the admins to use alongside the application."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["User pool groups can have IAM roles. The identity pool can choose the role from the token, which uses the cognito:preferred_role claim, so admins get the write role and other users get the default authenticated role.",
      "Two identity pools add complexity and make the application decide who gets which permissions.",
      "Application checks can be bypassed, because every user would hold write credentials.",
      "IAM users for admins add long-term keys and a separate path to manage."] },

{ id: "d4-15", d: 4,
  q: "A REST API in Amazon API Gateway serves a web application whose users sign in with an Amazon Cognito user pool. The API must reject requests that do not include a valid token from that user pool, without custom authorization code.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a Cognito user pool authorizer for the API. Set it on each method that needs it.",
      "Write a Lambda authorizer that downloads the user pool keys and checks each token's signature.",
      "Add an API key to the API. Give the API key to each user after the user signs in to the app.",
      "Add an AWS WAF rule that allows requests only when the Authorization header is present and set."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Cognito user pool authorizer validates the tokens that the user pool issues, without custom code. API Gateway rejects requests that have no valid token.",
      "A Lambda authorizer works, but it is the custom code that the company wants to avoid.",
      "API keys are not an authentication mechanism, and sharing them with users exposes them.",
      "AWS WAF can check that a header exists but cannot validate the token."] },

{ id: "d4-16", d: 4,
  q: "A script assumes role A, then uses role A's credentials to assume role B in another account and requests a session duration of 4 hours. Role B's maximum session duration is set to 12 hours, but the second AssumeRole call fails.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["Role chaining limits the session to 1 hour, so a request for 4 hours fails when the script uses role A's credentials.",
      "Role B's maximum session duration must be set to exactly 4 hours before a request for 4 hours can work for any caller.",
      "Role A must have the AdministratorAccess policy before it can assume a role in another account with a longer session.",
      "The second account must turn on AWS STS in the Region before the script can assume a role from another account there."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Using one role's credentials to assume another role is role chaining, which limits the session to a maximum of 1 hour, whatever the role's maximum session duration is.",
      "A request below the role's maximum is allowed for a normal assumption. The limit here comes from role chaining.",
      "Administrator access does not change the role chaining limit.",
      "STS is available in the Region, and the error comes from the requested duration."] },

{ id: "d4-17", d: 4,
  q: "Employees federate into a shared role, and then some employees assume other roles from that role. The security team must be able to identify in AWS CloudTrail which employee performed each action, even after the employees assume other roles.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Have the identity provider set a source identity with each employee's user name when the employee federates.",
      "Require the employees to type their names in the RoleSessionName field each time that they assume another role.",
      "Create a separate IAM user for each employee. Stop using federation for every one of the employees' sessions.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail Insights, which records the name of each employee who performed each role action in the logs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A source identity is set when the session starts and cannot be changed in later role sessions. It is recorded in CloudTrail for actions in the session and in roles assumed from it, so actions trace back to the employee.",
      "Session names are chosen by the caller and can be changed in each new session.",
      "IAM users add long-term credentials and remove the benefits of federation.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity rates. It does not add identity information."] },

{ id: "d4-18", d: 4,
  q: "Pods in an Amazon EKS cluster use the node's instance role, so every pod has the same AWS permissions. Each application must have only its own AWS permissions, without long-term credentials.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Install the EKS Pod Identity Agent. Create a Pod Identity association between each service account and its IAM role.",
      "Store a separate IAM user's access keys in a Kubernetes secret for each application. Mount the secret in each of its pods.",
      "Add all of the applications' permissions to the node instance role. Use Kubernetes RBAC to control AWS access.",
      "Run each application on its own node group. Give each node group an instance role with that application's needs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EKS Pod Identity gives pods credentials for the IAM role that is associated with their service account. Each application gets only its own role's permissions, with temporary credentials.",
      "Access keys in secrets are long-term credentials to rotate.",
      "Kubernetes RBAC controls Kubernetes API access, not AWS API access, so every pod would still have all permissions.",
      "Separate node groups work but add cost and management overhead."] },

{ id: "d4-19", d: 4,
  q: "A company manages access to its Amazon EKS clusters by editing the aws-auth ConfigMap. A bad edit recently locked administrators out of a cluster. The company wants to manage which IAM principals can access the Kubernetes API through the Amazon EKS API instead.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Change the cluster authentication mode to use EKS access entries. Create access entries with access policies.",
      "Keep the aws-auth ConfigMap, and give every developer the cluster creator's IAM role to avoid any lockouts later.",
      "Create an IAM user for each administrator. Add the users to the aws-auth ConfigMap with system:masters.",
      "Turn off authentication on the cluster endpoint. Restrict access to the endpoint with security groups."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access entries let you grant IAM principals access to the cluster through the EKS API, with EKS access policies or Kubernetes groups. They remove the need to edit the aws-auth ConfigMap.",
      "Sharing the cluster creator's role gives everyone full access and does not fix the process.",
      "This still depends on the ConfigMap and adds long-term credentials.",
      "Turning off authentication is not possible and would be insecure."] },

{ id: "d4-20", d: 4,
  q: "Users of a mobile app sign in with an Amazon Cognito user pool, and the app exchanges the token through a Cognito identity pool. After a migration to a new identity pool, AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity calls fail for all users. The IAM role is unchanged.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The role's trust policy checks cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:aud against the ID of the old identity pool.",
      "The user pool must be deleted and recreated before it can be used with a new identity pool for the app.",
      "The role needs the AmazonCognitoPowerUser managed policy before the new identity pool can use the role.",
      "Identity pools cannot use roles that were created before the identity pool, so every role must be recreated."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The trust policy for an identity pool role normally has a condition on cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:aud with the identity pool ID. With the old ID, the role no longer trusts tokens from the new pool.",
      "The user pool can be used with a new identity pool without being recreated.",
      "Permissions policies do not affect who can assume the role. The trust policy does.",
      "Existing roles can be used by a new identity pool when the trust policy is updated."] },

{ id: "d4-21", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses the IAM Identity Center directory as its identity source. A new policy requires all users to provide an MFA code each time that they sign in to the AWS access portal.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Set the IAM Identity Center MFA setting to prompt users for MFA every time they sign in (always-on).",
      "Attach an IAM policy to each permission set that denies all actions when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false.",
      "Turn on MFA for the root user of each member account in the organization. Keep the current portal settings.",
      "Ask the users to register an MFA device. Let each user decide when to use the device for portal sign-in."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IAM Identity Center can prompt users for MFA only when the sign-in context changes, or every time they sign in. The always-on setting requires an MFA code at every sign-in.",
      "An IAM condition in permission sets does not control the portal sign-in prompt.",
      "Root user MFA protects the root user, not portal users.",
      "Optional MFA does not meet a requirement for every sign-in."] },

{ id: "d4-22", d: 4,
  q: "A company's GitHub Actions workflows deploy to AWS by using IAM user access keys stored as repository secrets. The company must remove the long-term keys, and only workflows from the company's main branch of one repository must be able to deploy.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an IAM OIDC identity provider for token.actions.githubusercontent.com in the account.",
      "Create a deployment role whose trust policy checks the token's sub claim for that repository and branch.",
      "Rotate the access keys every 7 days. Update the repository secrets each time the keys are rotated.",
      "Create an IAM user for each workflow. Attach a permissions boundary to each of the IAM users.",
      "Make the deployment role trust all GitHub tokens. Check the branch name in the deployment script."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["An OIDC identity provider lets IAM trust tokens from GitHub, so workflows can call AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity and get temporary credentials.",
      "A condition on the sub claim limits the role to workflows from the named repository and branch, so other repositories and branches cannot assume it.",
      "Rotated keys are still long-term credentials stored in GitHub.",
      "IAM users still need long-term access keys.",
      "Trusting all GitHub tokens lets any repository assume the role, and checks in a script can be bypassed."] },

{ id: "d4-23", d: 4,
  q: "A developer's AWS CLI calls to assume a role fail with AccessDenied. The role's trust policy allows the developer's account only when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is true. The developer has an MFA device and uses access keys with the AWS CLI.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Have the developer pass the MFA device serial number and a current code when the developer assumes the role.",
      "Remove the MFA condition from the trust policy. Require the developer to sign in to the console instead.",
      "Attach the IAMFullAccess managed policy to the developer. Have the developer assume the role again.",
      "Create new access keys for the developer. Assign a second MFA device to the developer's IAM user account as a backup."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Requests signed with access keys alone are not MFA authenticated. When the developer passes the MFA serial number and token code, AWS STS checks the code, and the session meets the aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent condition.",
      "Removing the condition weakens the control that the trust policy enforces.",
      "No permissions policy can bypass the condition in the trust policy.",
      "New keys and a second device do not add MFA context to the request."] },

{ id: "d4-24", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 30 project teams, and new teams start every month. Each team must be able to start and stop only the Amazon EC2 instances that belong to its own project. The company wants to avoid writing a new policy for each new team.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Tag each instance and each principal with a project tag. Use one policy that allows the actions when aws:ResourceTag/project matches aws:PrincipalTag/project.",
      "Create a separate IAM policy for each team that lists the instance IDs of the team's instances. Update the policy when instances change.",
      "Create one IAM role for each team. Add each team's instance ARNs to the role's policy when the team launches new instances.",
      "Put each team's instances in a separate VPC. Use security groups to control which team can start and stop the instances."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) compares tags on the principal with tags on the resource. One policy works for every team, including new teams, as long as the tags are set.",
      "Per-team policies with instance IDs need constant updates as instances change and as teams are added.",
      "Per-team roles with instance ARNs are role-based access control that needs a change for each team and instance.",
      "Security groups control network traffic, not who can call the EC2 API."] },

{ id: "d4-25", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS IAM Identity Center with an external identity provider (IdP). The IdP stores each employee's cost center. IAM policies must grant access to resources based on the employee's cost center without a separate permission set for each cost center.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on attributes for access control in IAM Identity Center, map the cost center attribute, and use it as aws:PrincipalTag in policies.",
      "Create a permission set for each cost center. Assign each permission set to a group of employees from that cost center by hand.",
      "Add the cost center to each employee's user name. Use the aws:username condition key to match the cost center in the policies.",
      "Ask each employee to tag the resources that the employee creates with the cost center. Review all of the tags every quarter for any errors."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attributes for access control pass user attributes from the identity source as session tags. Policies in permission sets can then compare aws:PrincipalTag/costcenter with resource tags, which is ABAC with one permission set.",
      "A permission set per cost center is the overhead that the company wants to avoid.",
      "aws:username is set for IAM users, not for IAM Identity Center sessions, and user names are not meant to carry attributes.",
      "Tagging resources does not give the sessions the cost center attribute to compare with."] },

{ id: "d4-26", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants developers to create IAM roles for their own Lambda functions without help from the security team. The developers must not be able to create a role with more permissions than a set limit, and must not be able to remove that limit.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Allow iam:CreateRole only when the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition key equals the approved boundary policy ARN.",
      "Deny the developers any change to the boundary policy. Deny removing the boundary from any role.",
      "Allow the developers iam:CreateRole on all resources. Review new roles every week for extra permissions.",
      "Attach the AdministratorAccess policy to the developers. Turn on CloudTrail to record each new role.",
      "Ask the developers to create roles only through the console. Turn off their programmatic access keys."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A permissions boundary sets the maximum permissions of a role. Requiring the boundary with the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition key means that every role the developers create is limited by it.",
      "The developers could otherwise edit the boundary policy or delete the boundary from a role, so those actions must be denied for the control to hold.",
      "Weekly reviews find roles with extra permissions after they exist.",
      "Administrator access lets the developers do anything, and logs do not prevent it.",
      "The console uses the same IAM APIs, so this does not limit what roles can do."] },

{ id: "d4-27", d: 4,
  q: "A multi-tenant application assumes one IAM role to read tenant data from an Amazon S3 bucket, with one prefix for each tenant. The company wants each request to be limited to the current tenant's prefix, even if the application code has a bug.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Pass a session policy in each AssumeRole call that allows access only to the current tenant's prefix.",
      "Create an IAM user for each tenant. Store each tenant user's access keys in the application.",
      "Grant the role access to all prefixes. Check the tenant ID in the application before each read.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning to make sure that any data read from the wrong tenant can be recovered later by the team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The permissions of a session are the intersection of the role's policies and the session policy. A session policy for each tenant limits each session to that tenant's prefix, whatever the code tries to read.",
      "IAM users for tenants add long-term credentials and do not scale.",
      "A check in code is the control that a bug could break.",
      "Versioning keeps object versions. It does not prevent reads across tenants."] },

{ id: "d4-28", d: 4,
  q: "A role in account B must read objects from an Amazon S3 bucket in account A. The objects are encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key in account A. The bucket policy allows the role, but the role gets AccessDenied errors on GetObject.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Update the KMS key policy in account A to allow account B, or the role, to use kms:Decrypt.",
      "Add kms:Decrypt on the key's ARN to the role's identity policy in account B for the reads.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to the AWS managed key aws/s3 for objects that exist.",
      "Grant the role access through an S3 object ACL in account A that names account B as owner.",
      "Turn off encryption on the bucket. Copy the objects again to make sure that they are unencrypted."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Cross-account use of a KMS key needs the key policy in the key's account to allow the other account or its principal.",
      "The principal in account B also needs an identity policy that allows kms:Decrypt on the key. Both sides must allow the action.",
      "The policy of an AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account, and existing objects keep their key.",
      "ACLs do not grant KMS permissions, and the bucket policy already allows the S3 access.",
      "Removing encryption weakens data protection instead of fixing the permissions."] },

{ id: "d4-29", d: 4,
  q: "An administrator in a member account of an organization in AWS Organizations has the AdministratorAccess policy. The administrator cannot create any resources in the eu-west-3 Region, and each call fails with an explicit deny. The administrator's IAM policies contain no deny statements.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["An SCP that applies to the account denies actions outside the company's approved Regions.",
      "The AdministratorAccess policy does not include permissions for Regions that opened after 2019.",
      "The eu-west-3 Region requires a separate IAM user that is created in that Region for each admin.",
      "AWS STS is turned off in the account, so the administrator's credentials work only in us-east-1."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SCPs set the maximum permissions for member accounts. An SCP that denies actions based on aws:RequestedRegion blocks the calls even for administrators, and the deny comes from the SCP, not from IAM policies.",
      "AdministratorAccess allows all actions on all resources in all Regions.",
      "IAM is a global service, and IAM users are not created for each Region.",
      "Turning off STS endpoints in a Region does not affect calls to other services with existing credentials."] },

{ id: "d4-30", d: 4,
  q: "A security engineer has written a new IAM policy for a group of analysts. Before the policy is attached, the engineer must confirm which Amazon S3 actions it allows on two specific buckets, and must find which statement allows or denies each action.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use the IAM Policy Simulator to test the policy against the actions and the bucket ARNs.",
      "Attach the policy to the group. Ask the analysts to report any actions that they cannot do.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for the buckets. Review the logs after the policy is attached.",
      "Run IAM Access Analyzer policy generation on the group before the new policy is attached to it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The IAM Policy Simulator evaluates policies against chosen actions and resources without making real requests. It reports whether each action is allowed or denied and which statement caused the result.",
      "Testing in production with real users is risky and slow.",
      "CloudTrail shows what happened after the policy is in use, not what the policy allows before it is attached.",
      "Policy generation creates a policy from past activity. It does not test a new policy."] },

{ id: "d4-31", d: 4,
  q: "A company has hundreds of accounts in AWS Organizations. The security team must find every S3 bucket, KMS key, IAM role, and other supported resource whose policy grants access to principals outside the organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust.",
      "Review the resource policies in every account each month with a script. Record outside principals.",
      "Turn on Amazon Macie in every account. Review its findings for resources that are shared outside.",
      "Use the IAM Policy Simulator in each account to test each resource policy against a list of outsiders."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust generates findings for supported resources that allow access from principals outside the organization.",
      "Custom scripts across hundreds of accounts are overhead and easy to get wrong.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data and some S3 access settings, not shared KMS keys or roles.",
      "The simulator tests policies one at a time and cannot list unknown outside principals."] },

{ id: "d4-32", d: 4,
  q: "A security team must find IAM roles that have not been used in 90 days, access keys that have not been used, and permissions that roles have but never use, across all accounts in the company's organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer for the organization with a 90-day tracking period.",
      "Download the IAM credential report in each account every month, and compare the dates in a spreadsheet.",
      "Search CloudTrail logs in each account for every action of every role, and list the roles with no events.",
      "Turn on AWS Config in each account. Use the IAM resource configuration history to find unused roles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unused access analyzer reports unused roles, unused access keys and passwords, and unused permissions across the organization, based on the tracking period that you set.",
      "Credential reports cover users and keys in one account at a time and do not show unused permissions in roles.",
      "Searching CloudTrail for every role is slow and complex at this scale.",
      "AWS Config records configuration changes, not whether roles or permissions are used."] },

{ id: "d4-33", d: 4,
  q: "An application team's IAM role has broad permissions that were granted during development. The application has run in production for 60 days. The security team must replace the policy with one that allows only the actions that the application actually uses.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate a policy from the role's activity in AWS CloudTrail, then review and attach it.",
      "Remove all permissions from the role. Add back each permission when the application fails in production.",
      "Ask the developers to list every API call that the application makes. Write a new policy from the list.",
      "Attach the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy to the role in place of the broad policy. Monitor for failures."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access Analyzer policy generation reviews the role's CloudTrail activity for a chosen period and generates a policy with the actions and services that were used. The team reviews and refines it before attaching it.",
      "Removing all permissions breaks production while permissions are added back.",
      "A manual list from memory is likely to miss or include actions.",
      "ReadOnlyAccess may break needed write actions and still allows reads that the application does not need."] },

{ id: "d4-34", d: 4,
  q: "Developers change IAM policies through a CI/CD pipeline. The security team wants the pipeline to fail automatically when a change to a policy grants any access that the current version of the policy does not grant.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add an IAM Access Analyzer custom policy check that runs CheckNoNewAccess against the current policy version.",
      "Add a manual approval step to the pipeline. Ask a security engineer to read each new version of the policy.",
      "Run the IAM Policy Simulator for every action in AWS after each deployment, and roll back any new access found.",
      "Turn on AWS Config rules for IAM policies. Send a notification after each policy change that is deployed."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The CheckNoNewAccess custom policy check compares a new policy with a reference policy, such as the current version, and fails when the new policy grants new access. The pipeline can run it before deployment.",
      "Manual review is slow and depends on people catching every change.",
      "Testing after deployment lets the new access exist in production first.",
      "AWS Config detects changes after deployment and does not compare access between versions."] },

{ id: "d4-35", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket stores software packages that every account in the company's organization must be able to read. New accounts are added often. The bucket must not be readable by accounts outside the organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that allows s3:GetObject when the aws:PrincipalOrgID condition matches the organization ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy that lists the account ID of every account in the organization. Update it for new accounts.",
      "Make the bucket public for reads. Share the bucket name only with the teams that work in the organization.",
      "Create an IAM user in the bucket's account for every other account. Share the access keys with the teams."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The aws:PrincipalOrgID condition key matches the organization of the requesting principal's account. One statement allows every current and future account in the organization and no outside accounts.",
      "A list of account IDs must be updated for each new account.",
      "A public bucket is readable by anyone who learns its name.",
      "Shared IAM users add long-term credentials and do not scale."] },

{ id: "d4-36", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon SNS topic receives event notifications from an Amazon S3 bucket. The topic policy allows the s3.amazonaws.com service principal to publish. The security team must ensure that only this bucket in the company's account can publish to the topic through the service.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add aws:SourceArn and aws:SourceAccount conditions for the bucket and the account to the statement for the service principal.",
      "Remove the service principal from the topic policy. Allow the IAM role of the bucket owner's account to publish instead.",
      "Add an aws:PrincipalOrgID condition for the company's organization ID to the topic policy statement for the service principal.",
      "Encrypt the topic with an AWS KMS key. Allow only the bucket's account to use the key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When a service principal is allowed, the aws:SourceArn and aws:SourceAccount condition keys limit the access to requests that the service makes for the named resource and account. This prevents the cross-service confused deputy problem.",
      "S3 publishes as the service principal, not as a role in the account, so notifications would stop.",
      "A service principal does not belong to an organization, so aws:PrincipalOrgID does not apply to it.",
      "Encryption protects message data. It does not control which bucket can publish."] },

{ id: "d4-37", d: 4,
  q: "A company is building a document management application. Permissions such as which users can view, edit, or share each document must be managed outside the application code, and must be easy to audit and change.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use Amazon Verified Permissions with a policy store and Cedar policies. Call it from the application for each decision.",
      "Store permissions in each document's metadata. Write code in the application to check the metadata for each request.",
      "Create an IAM role for each document. Have the application assume the matching role before each document request.",
      "Hardcode the permission rules in the application. Redeploy the whole application each time that a permission rule changes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Verified Permissions is a managed service for fine-grained authorization in custom applications. Policies are written in Cedar and stored outside the code, and the application asks for an authorization decision for each request.",
      "Custom permission logic in code is hard to audit and change.",
      "IAM roles are not meant to model application-level permissions on documents, and there are quotas on roles.",
      "Hardcoded rules need a redeployment for every change."] },

{ id: "d4-38", d: 4,
  q: "Team A's lead must be able to create, update, and delete IAM roles for team A's applications, but must not be able to change roles that belong to other teams. All of team A's roles are created under the /team-a/ path.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Allow the IAM role actions only on the resource arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/team-a/*.",
      "Allow the IAM role actions on all roles. Ask the lead not to change roles of other teams.",
      "Create a separate account for each team. Give the lead AdministratorAccess in all of them.",
      "Allow the IAM role actions only when aws:RequestedRegion matches the Region where team A works."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IAM paths are part of a role's ARN. A policy that allows the actions only on roles under /team-a/ limits the lead to team A's roles.",
      "A request to the lead is not a control.",
      "Administrator access in every account is far more than the lead needs.",
      "IAM is a global service, so a Region condition does not separate the teams' roles."] },

{ id: "d4-39", d: 4,
  q: "Developers launch Amazon EC2 instances for their applications. They must be able to attach only the app-instance role to the instances, and must not be able to attach any other role, such as an administrator role.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Allow iam:PassRole only on the app-instance role's ARN, with iam:PassedToService set to ec2.amazonaws.com.",
      "Allow iam:PassRole on all roles. Turn on CloudTrail to find any instance that has an administrator role attached.",
      "Allow iam:CreateRole for the developers to let each developer create the role that the instance needs.",
      "Deny ec2:RunInstances for the developers. Ask the security team to launch every instance on request."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attaching a role to an instance requires iam:PassRole on that role. Allowing PassRole only on the app-instance role, and only to the EC2 service, prevents the developers from passing any other role.",
      "Detecting the misuse later does not prevent it.",
      "CreateRole would let the developers create roles with any permissions.",
      "Denying launches blocks the developers' work instead of limiting the role."] },

{ id: "d4-40", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon EC2 API call fails with an UnauthorizedOperation error that includes an encoded authorization message. The security engineer must see the details of why the request was denied, such as which policy statement caused the deny.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Call sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage with the encoded message, with a principal that has permission for that action.",
      "Paste the encoded message into the IAM Policy Simulator. Run the simulation for the action that failed.",
      "Search the CloudTrail logs for the request, and read the encoded message in the event, which is shown in clear text.",
      "Open an AWS Support case to decode the message. Wait for the support team to explain the reason for the deny."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS STS DecodeAuthorizationMessage API decodes the message and shows details such as the matched statements and the context of the request. The caller needs permission for sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage.",
      "The Policy Simulator does not accept encoded messages.",
      "The encoded message is not decoded in CloudTrail events.",
      "The engineer can decode the message directly without waiting for AWS Support."] },

{ id: "d4-41", d: 4,
  q: "A Lambda function in account A must be invoked by an application role in account B. The role in account B has an identity policy that allows lambda:InvokeFunction on the function's ARN, but the calls fail with AccessDenied.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Add a statement to the function's resource-based policy that allows account B's role to invoke the function.",
      "Add lambda:InvokeFunction to the function's execution role in account A.",
      "Move the function into a VPC in account A, and peer that VPC with the application's VPC in account B for access.",
      "Create an IAM user in account A for the application. Share the user's access keys with the team in account B."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account invocation needs a resource-based policy on the function that allows the other account or role. Both the identity policy in account B and the resource-based policy in account A must allow the call.",
      "The execution role controls what the function can do, not who can invoke it.",
      "Lambda invocation is an API call, so network peering does not grant permission.",
      "Shared IAM user keys add long-term credentials instead of using the cross-account policy."] },

{ id: "d4-42", d: 4,
  q: "A shared Amazon S3 data lake bucket is used by 25 teams, and the bucket policy has become too large and complex to manage. Each team must have its own access rules for its own prefix, managed separately.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an S3 access point for each team with its own access point policy, and delegate access control from the bucket policy.",
      "Create a separate bucket for each team. Copy the shared data into each of the 25 team buckets each night by using a job.",
      "Keep one bucket policy, and split it into 25 statements that the security team updates whenever any of the teams asks for it.",
      "Grant every team full access to the bucket. Trust each team to work only with the objects under its own prefix of data."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 access points are named network endpoints for a bucket, each with its own policy. The bucket policy can delegate access control to the access points, so each team's rules are managed separately.",
      "Copying data to 25 buckets adds cost and consistency problems.",
      "A single growing bucket policy is the problem that the company wants to solve, and it has a size limit.",
      "Full access for every team breaks least privilege."] },

{ id: "d4-43", d: 4,
  q: "A developer's identity policy allows sqs:SendMessage on a queue. The developer's IAM user also has a permissions boundary that allows only Amazon S3 and Amazon EC2 actions. The developer's calls to send messages to the queue fail.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The permissions boundary does not allow sqs:SendMessage, so the action is not allowed.",
      "The queue's encryption setting is blocking every message that the developer sends to it.",
      "Permissions boundaries apply only to roles, so the policy on the IAM user is ignored now.",
      "The developer must sign in with MFA before any SQS action is allowed for an IAM user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The effective permissions of a principal with a permissions boundary are the intersection of its identity policies and its boundary. The boundary does not include SQS, so the action is implicitly denied.",
      "Nothing in the scenario points to encryption.",
      "Permissions boundaries apply to IAM users and roles.",
      "Nothing in the policies requires MFA."] },

{ id: "d4-44", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket policy denies all S3 actions when the aws:SourceVpce condition key does not match the company's VPC endpoint. An administrator who has the AdministratorAccess policy cannot list the bucket's objects in the AWS Management Console.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The console request does not come through the VPC endpoint, so the explicit deny in the bucket policy applies.",
      "The AdministratorAccess policy does not include the s3:ListBucket action that the console needs to list objects.",
      "The console can only list objects in buckets that have S3 Block Public Access turned off for the whole account.",
      "The bucket is in a different Region than the console, so the console cannot list any of the bucket's objects."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An explicit deny in any applicable policy overrides every allow. Requests from the administrator's browser do not come through the VPC endpoint, so the deny statement matches and blocks them.",
      "AdministratorAccess allows all S3 actions.",
      "Block Public Access does not affect authenticated access from the console.",
      "The console can list buckets and objects in all Regions."] },

{ id: "d4-45", d: 4,
  q: "An application role in account B must read a secret in AWS Secrets Manager in account A. The secret is encrypted with the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager. The role's identity policy already allows secretsmanager:GetSecretValue and kms:Decrypt on the secret and key.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Encrypt the secret with a customer managed key whose key policy allows the role to use kms:Decrypt.",
      "Attach a resource-based policy to the secret that allows the role in account B to get the secret value.",
      "Add the role from account B to the key policy of the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager in account A.",
      "Replicate the secret to account B by using the replication feature of Secrets Manager to that account.",
      "Make the secret public for reads. Restrict reads with an aws:SourceIp condition for account B."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The key policy of an AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account, so the secret must use a customer managed key that allows the role.",
      "Cross-account access to a secret also needs a resource-based policy on the secret that allows the role in the other account.",
      "AWS managed key policies cannot be edited.",
      "Secrets Manager replication copies secrets to other Regions, not to other accounts.",
      "Secrets cannot be made public, and IP conditions do not grant cross-account access."] },

{ id: "d4-46", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses ABAC. Policies allow principals to manage Amazon EC2 instances when the instance's project tag matches the principal's project tag. The security team finds that some users changed tags to get access to other projects' instances.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Deny ec2:CreateTags and ec2:DeleteTags on the project tag key, by using aws:TagKeys, for everyone except tag admins.",
      "Deny iam:TagUser, iam:TagRole, and iam:UntagRole to the users to stop them from changing their own principal tags.",
      "Switch from tags to instance IDs in the policies. Update the policies by hand each time an instance launches.",
      "Turn on AWS Config to record tag changes. Review the configuration history at the end of each month for abuse.",
      "Move each project's instances to a separate Region. Add an aws:RequestedRegion condition to each user's policy."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["If users can change the project tag on instances, they can move instances into their own project. Denying tag changes on that key, with aws:TagKeys, protects the resource side of ABAC.",
      "If users can change their own principal tags, they can join any project. Denying IAM tagging actions protects the principal side of ABAC.",
      "Instance IDs in policies remove the benefits of ABAC and need constant updates.",
      "Monthly reviews detect abuse after it has happened.",
      "Separate Regions for projects add complexity and do not stop tag changes."] },

{ id: "d4-47", d: 4,
  q: "Users of an application sign in through an Amazon Cognito user pool, and an identity pool gives them AWS credentials. Each user has a department attribute. IAM policies must allow access to Amazon S3 objects only when the object's department tag matches the user's department.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on attributes for access control in the identity pool, map the department attribute to a principal tag, and use aws:PrincipalTag in the role's policy.",
      "Create one identity pool for each department, with a separate IAM role for each department. Route each user to the pool for the department.",
      "Give every user access to all objects. Check the department in the application before each request that the user makes to Amazon S3.",
      "Create an IAM user for each department. Give each user of the application the access keys for the IAM user of the user's department."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Identity pools can map user attributes from the identity provider to principal tags on the session. The role's policy then compares aws:PrincipalTag/department with the object's tag, which is ABAC with one role.",
      "Separate pools and roles per department add overhead as departments change.",
      "Checks in the application can be bypassed, because every user would hold full credentials.",
      "Shared IAM user keys are long-term credentials and cannot be traced to individual users."] },

{ id: "d4-48", d: 4,
  q: "IAM Access Analyzer reports findings for an Amazon S3 bucket that hosts a public website. Public access to this bucket is intended. The security team wants these findings, and findings for future buckets with the same website tag, not to appear as active.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an Access Analyzer archive rule that matches the public website bucket's criteria to archive the findings automatically.",
      "Delete the external access analyzer. Create a new analyzer each time that the team wants to review the findings again.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the bucket. Keep the public website running in place.",
      "Ask the team to archive each finding by hand in the console each time that a new finding appears for any website bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Archive rules automatically archive new findings that match the criteria that you define, so findings for intended access do not stay active.",
      "Deleting the analyzer removes monitoring for all other resources.",
      "Block Public Access would break the public website.",
      "Archiving by hand each time is manual work that an archive rule removes."] },

{ id: "d4-49", d: 4,
  q: "Employees federate to AWS through a SAML identity provider (IdP) and can assume a shared role. The role's policies use ABAC with aws:PrincipalTag/project. The employees can sign in, but every action on their project's resources is denied.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The IdP is not sending the project attribute as a PrincipalTag SAML attribute, so the session has no project tag.",
      "The role's trust policy is missing the SAML provider, so the employees cannot assume the role at all.",
      "ABAC works only with IAM users, so the shared role cannot use principal tags in any of its policies.",
      "The resources must be in the same Region as the IdP for tag-based conditions to match the session."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session tags for SAML federation come from attributes that the IdP sends, named https://aws.amazon.com/SAML/Attributes/PrincipalTag:project. If the IdP does not send the attribute, aws:PrincipalTag/project has no value, and the conditions fail.",
      "The employees can sign in and assume the role, so the trust policy works.",
      "Roles support principal tags and session tags for ABAC.",
      "IdPs are not tied to a Region, and tags do not depend on the Region."] },

{ id: "d4-50", d: 4,
  q: "A policy denies all actions when aws:SourceIp is not in the corporate IP range. Since the policy was added, users in the office can no longer deploy AWS CloudFormation stacks, because the calls that CloudFormation makes for the users are denied.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Add the aws:ViaAWSService condition key with a value of false to the deny statement.",
      "Add the AWS IP address ranges of every Region to the list of allowed corporate ranges.",
      "Remove the users' permissions for CloudFormation. Deploy every stack from the console.",
      "Replace aws:SourceIp with aws:RequestedRegion in the deny statement for the AWS Regions."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When a service such as CloudFormation calls other services with the user's credentials, the source IP is the service's address. Adding aws:ViaAWSService false means that the deny applies only to direct calls from users, not to calls that a service makes for them.",
      "Allowing all AWS ranges is broad, the ranges change often, and other customers use those addresses.",
      "Console calls still go through the same CloudFormation behavior.",
      "A Region condition does not replace the network control that the company wants."] },
]);
